; The name the interception scenario is asked about - S-1.
;
; WHY A PUBLIC-NAMESPACE NAME RATHER THAN ANOTHER .test ONE. The whole of S-1 is
; "the browser works and npm install does not", and that story is about a name
; somebody fetches packages from. A .test name reads as an internal service, and
; an internal service under a private certificate authority is ORDINARY - which
; is the one reading this scenario must not invite.
;
; example.net IS RESERVED FOR EXACTLY THIS BY RFC 2606, so the bench answers for
; a name that belongs to nobody, and docs/14 requires that: a testbed certificate
; is never minted for a real company's name, not even in a scenario pretending to
; be a particular vendor's interception. registry.example.net evokes the package
; registry the scenario is about without naming somebody else's service, which is
; the substitution docs/21 already made for S-1.
;
; THE ADDRESS IS THE MIDDLEBOX, NOT THE ORIGIN, and that is the scenario. The
; origin it re-signs for lives at 172.28.0.80 and answers to partner.test. Both
; addresses are inside the bench's own subnet and unreachable from anywhere else.
$ORIGIN example.net.
$TTL 60

@   IN SOA ns.example.net. admin.example.net. (
        1          ; serial
        7200       ; refresh
        3600       ; retry
        1209600    ; expire
        3600 )     ; minimum

@   IN NS  ns.example.net.
ns  IN A   172.28.0.53

; The middlebox. It terminates TLS, mints a certificate for whatever name was
; asked for, and forwards to the origin - S-1.
registry  IN A   172.28.0.81
